window.PF_INDICE=[{"t": "One question, five answers", "p": "inicio", "a": "tu-nivel", "k": "That's how the whole site works: the same idea told for every age. Try the colored buttons. Why doesn't the Moon fall onto the Earth? It is falling! But it moves sideways so fast that, as it falls, the Earth «curves away» beneath it. It's like swinging a ball tied to a string: the string is gravity. Earth's gravity pulls on the Moon all the time. Because the Moon also moves sideways at about 1 km/s, instead of crashing it follows a curve around us: an orbit. Newton realized it is the same force that makes an apple fall. The Moon is in free fall: the gravitational pull acts as the centripetal force, . With km you get km/s and a period of about 27.3 days, just what is observed. It's a two-body problem with potential : the energy is negative, so the orbit is bound (an ellipse with eccentricity ≈ 0.055). Conserved angular momentum keeps from going to 0. In general relativity the Moon follows"}, {"t": "What are you curious about today?", "p": "inicio", "a": "curiosidad", "k": "Start with a question. Each one leads to the explanation and an experiment. Why is the sky blue?And why sunsets are red, with the same answer. What's inside a black hole?A place not even light escapes, drawn on a map. How does a steel ship float?Archimedes' buoyancy, with blocks you can sink. Can time run slower?Yes: your GPS corrects for it every day thanks to Einstein. What are you made of, deep down?Quarks, electrons and a field that gives mass. How does a satellite stay in orbit?It falls, but never lands. Fire Newton's cannon. Can something be in two places at once?Send particles one by one through two slits. How did it all begin?From the Big Bang to you, squeezed into a single year."}, {"t": "Experiment of the day", "p": "inicio", "a": "del-dia", "k": "See all 35 experiments"}, {"t": "Your stamp book", "p": "inicio", "a": "sellos", "k": "Every topic ends with a quiz. Get at least 60% right and you earn its stamp. Your progress is saved in this browser."}, {"t": "Keep exploring", "p": "inicio", "a": "sigue", "k": "Start herePhysicsWhat it is, what it's for, its big branches with explorable maps, and where to study it in Mexico. 73 momentsTimelineFrom Thales of Miletus rubbing amber to the 2026 Nobel Prize. 30 storiesPhysicists and their workWho overlapped with whom, and two Mexicans who changed physics. GameMyth or fact?Lightning never strikes the same place twice? Test yourself. 18 discoveriesWhat physics is your phone made of?Pick an object and see which discoveries made it possible. ExercisesHigh-school guideProblems generated at random that grade themselves, plus a formula sheet."}, {"t": "Quick challenge", "p": "inicio", "a": "reto", "k": "«The eternally incomprehensible thing about the world is its comprehensibility.»Albert Einstein, «Physik und Realität» (1936)"}, {"t": "What is physics?", "p": "fisica", "a": "que-es", "k": "Physics is the science of «why?» questions. Why does a ball fall? Why does a rainbow have colors? Why does a magnet pull a paper clip? Physicists observe, play with experiments and discover the secret rules that everything follows: from a swing to the stars. Physics studies matter, energy, space and time, and how they relate. It uses experiments and mathematics to find laws: rules that always work, in your home and in a distant galaxy. For example, the same gravity that makes an apple fall keeps the Moon going around the Earth. Physics is the natural science that studies the properties of matter and energy and the interactions between them, from subatomic particles to galaxies. Its method combines observation, experiment and mathematical reasoning: models are proposed, quantitative predictions are made and compared with measurements. If a model fails, it is corrected or replaced. Physics"}, {"t": "The map of physics", "p": "fisica", "a": "mapa", "k": "Physics is divided into branches that connect with one another. Open each box to see what it studies and jump to the full explanation. Physics The science of matter, energy, space and time. Classical mechanics Kinematics Newton's laws Forces change motion. Inertia, F = ma and action–reaction. Energy and work Energy changes form but is never created or destroyed. Gravitation Every mass attracts every other mass. It explains falls, tides and orbits. Forces and friction Normal force, tension, weight and the friction that resists sliding. Oscillations and waves Springs, pendulums, strings and sound: everything that vibrates. Thermodynamics Heat, temperature and energy in systems with enormous numbers of particles. Heat and temperature Laws of thermodynamics Energy is conserved and the entropy of an isolated system does not decrease. Heat engines Engines and refrigerators; no machine beats th"}, {"t": "What is physics for?", "p": "fisica", "a": "para-que", "k": "Almost everything you use every day exists because someone understood a physical law. Some examples: Your phone's GPSThe satellites carry atomic clocks. Without the corrections from special and general relativity, the position would drift by several kilometers every day.Relativity Chips and computersTransistors work thanks to how electrons behave in semiconductors, something only quantum mechanics explains.Quantum physics · condensed matter Magnetic resonance imagingThe hydrogen nuclei in your body behave like tiny magnets; with magnetic fields and radio waves you get images without surgery.Electromagnetism · nuclear physics Solar and wind powerSolar cells use the photoelectric effect; wind turbines use Faraday's electromagnetic induction.Quantum · electromagnetism Fiber-optic internetLight travels along glass threads, bouncing by total internal reflection, carrying data between continen"}, {"t": "Why does it matter?", "p": "fisica", "a": "importancia", "k": "Because it helps us understand the world and invent amazing things: airplanes, phones, vaccines kept cold, rockets that reach the Moon. And because it's great fun to wonder how everything works! Understanding the universe. It provides a framework to explain natural phenomena, from rainbows to black holes. Technological development. Most modern technologies come from advances in physics: electricity, telecommunications, electronics, medical imaging. Critical thinking. It teaches you to form hypotheses, measure, estimate errors and decide with evidence: skills useful in any career. The basis of other sciences. Chemistry, biology, geology and engineering rely on physical laws. Global challenges. Clean energy, climate change and new materials all require physics. Beyond its usefulness, physics is the most successful attempt to describe nature with a minimal number of principles. Its epistemi"}, {"t": "The major branches, one by one", "p": "fisica", "a": "ramas", "k": "Each branch has its explanation by level, its key discoveries, its formulas and an experiment to play with. Mechanics Mechanics studies how things move: why a ball rolls, why you lurch forward when the bus brakes, and why it's harder to push a refrigerator than a shoebox. Mechanics studies the motion of bodies and the forces that cause it. Isaac Newton summed it up in three laws: objects keep their motion if nothing pushes them (inertia), a force produces an acceleration (F = m·a), and every force has an equal and opposite reaction. Classical mechanics is divided into kinematics (describes motion with position, velocity and acceleration), dynamics (relates forces and motion through Newton's laws) and statics (equilibrium). Together with the law of universal gravitation, it explains everything from falling bodies to planetary orbits. Beyond Newton, mechanics is reformulated with the Lagra"}, {"t": "Being a physicist", "p": "fisica", "a": "ser-fisico", "k": ""}, {"t": "Why study physics?", "p": "fisica", "a": "por-que-estudiar", "k": "Curiosity. You work on the deepest questions: what everything is made of, how the universe began, what time is. Versatility. People who study physics learn to model, program, measure and solve hard problems; that's why they work in research, industry, finance, data science, medicine, energy and education. Impact. Clean energy, medical diagnosis, quantum computing, new materials: physics is behind the big challenges. Transferable skills. Mathematics, programming, data analysis and critical thinking. «Look up at the stars and not down at your feet. Try to make sense of what you see, and wonder about what makes the universe exist.»Stephen Hawking, opening ceremony of the London 2012 Paralympic Games"}, {"t": "How much does a physicist earn?", "p": "fisica", "a": "cuanto-gana", "k": "It depends a lot on the country, specialty, degree (bachelor's, master's or PhD) and sector. As a general reference, approximate annual ranges in the United States are: General estimates for the U.S. (USD per year, before taxes) SectorApproximate rangeExample jobs Academic research40 000 – 100 000Postdocs, professors, research institutes Tech industry and energy60 000 – 120 000+Semiconductors, optics, energy, data science Medical physics70 000 – 150 000Radiotherapy, imaging, radiation protection Government and national laboratories60 000 – 130 000Metrology, energy, space, defense These are rough figures and change over time. In Mexico salaries are usually lower and vary a lot between academia (for example, with support from the National System of Researchers) and industry. For up-to-date data by degree you can check IMCO's Compara Carreras portal."}, {"t": "What can I study and what can I specialize in?", "p": "fisica", "a": "especialidades", "k": "Specialties Some of the areas where people who study physics work. Theoretical physics Experimental physics Designs experiments and detectors; measures with precision. Computational physics Numerical simulations and big-data analysis; a growing field. Astrophysics and cosmology Stars, galaxies, black holes, gravitational waves. Particle physics Condensed matter and materials Semiconductors, superconductors, nanomaterials and coatings. Medical physics Radiotherapy, imaging, nuclear medicine. Optics and photonics Lasers, optical fibers, sensors. Quantum information Quantum computing, cryptography and sensors. Applied physics and energy Renewable energy, fusion, energy storage. Teaching and outreach Training the next generations and bringing science to everyone."}, {"t": "Where to study physics in Mexico", "p": "fisica", "a": "donde-mexico", "k": "Some of the best-known institutions with undergraduate or graduate physics programs (listed by state; the order is not a ranking): IPN — Escuela Superior de Física y Matemáticas (ESFM)Mexico City. Bachelor's in Physics and Mathematics and in Mathematical Engineering; graduate programs in physics. UNAM — Facultad de CienciasMexico City. Bachelor's in Physics and Biomedical Physics; institutes of Physics, Nuclear Sciences and Astronomy. UAM IztapalapaMexico City. Undergraduate and graduate physics. CinvestavGraduate programs in physics (Zacatenco, Mérida and other campuses), strong in particles, condensed matter and applied physics. BUAP — Facultad de Ciencias Físico MatemáticasPuebla. Bachelor's in Physics and Applied Physics. Universidad de GuanajuatoLeón. Division of Sciences and Engineering. Universidad de Guadalajara — CUCEIJalisco. Bachelor's in Physics. UANL — Facultad de Ciencias F"}, {"t": "Some leading universities around the world", "p": "fisica", "a": "donde-mundo", "k": "MITUnited StatesCaltechUnited StatesStanfordUnited StatesHarvardUnited States PrincetonUnited StatesUC BerkeleyUnited StatesUniversity of ChicagoUnited States University of CambridgeUnited KingdomUniversity of OxfordUnited Kingdom ETH ZurichSwitzerlandUniversity of TokyoJapanMax Planck Institute / LMU MunichGermany International rankings change every year; these lists are a guide, not a ranking."}, {"t": "Sources and further reading", "p": "fisica", "a": "fuentes", "k": "Serway, R. A. and Jewett, J. W. Physics for Scientists and Engineers. Cengage. Halliday, D., Resnick, R. and Walker, J. Fundamentals of Physics. Wiley. Young, H. D. and Freedman, R. A. University Physics (Sears–Zemansky). Pearson. Feynman, R. P., Leighton, R. B. and Sands, M. The Feynman Lectures on Physics (free to read online at feynmanlectures.caltech.edu). Griffiths, D. J. Introduction to Electrodynamics. Cambridge University Press. Einstein, A. Relativity: The Special and the General Theory. Planck Collaboration (2018). Cosmological parameters. Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics 641, A6. Particle Data Group. Review of Particle Physics (pdg.lbl.gov). To keep learning: how to study physics and resources by level."}, {"t": "Mechanics (branch)", "p": "fisica", "a": "r-mecanica", "k": ""}, {"t": "Thermodynamics (branch)", "p": "fisica", "a": "r-termo", "k": ""}, {"t": "Electromagnetism (branch)", "p": "fisica", "a": "r-em", "k": ""}, {"t": "Optics (branch)", "p": "fisica", "a": "r-optica", "k": ""}, {"t": "Modern physics (branch)", "p": "fisica", "a": "r-moderna", "k": ""}, {"t": "Origin, evolution and fate of the universe and of humanity", "p": "fisica", "a": "r-cosmos", "k": ""}, {"t": "Basic", "p": "temas", "a": "basico", "k": "The foundations: motion, forces, energy, gravity, light and sound."}, {"t": "Newton's laws", "p": "temas", "a": "newton", "k": "From Explorer Imagine you're in a shopping cart. If nobody pushes it, it stays still. If you push it, it moves, and if you push harder, it speeds up faster. And if you push a wall… the wall pushes you back! Those are the three rules Isaac Newton discovered more than 300 years ago. Isaac Newton (1642–1727) described motion with three laws: Inertia: an object stays at rest or keeps moving in a straight line at constant speed as long as no net force acts on it. That's why you lurch forward when the bus brakes. F = m·a: a force produces an acceleration; the more mass an object has, the more force it takes to accelerate it by the same amount. Action and reaction: if A pushes B, B pushes A with the same force in the opposite direction. Rockets move forward by pushing gases backward. Newton's laws, published in the Principia (1687), are the foundation of classical mechanics. The first defines i"}, {"t": "Energy and work", "p": "temas", "a": "energia-trabajo", "k": "From Explorer Energy is what makes things happen: you running, a light bulb shining, a car moving. It comes in many forms: energy of motion, energy stored in height, in food, in batteries… and it can always be changed from one form to another! Energy is a system's ability to do work. Work is done when a force moves something: if you push a box along a hallway, you transfer energy to it. A moving object has kinetic energy and a raised one has gravitational potential energy. Both are measured in joules (J). The work done by a constant force is , where θ is the angle between the force and the displacement: if the force is perpendicular to the motion, it does no work. The work–energy theorem says that the net work on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy, . Power is the rate at which work is done, , measured in watts (W). For variable forces, . If the force is conservative ( ), the "}, {"t": "Gravity", "p": "temas", "a": "gravedad", "k": "From Explorer Gravity is the invisible force that makes things fall and keeps us stuck to the ground. Everything attracts everything! You also pull on the Earth, but it's so big that it wins. Gravity also keeps the Moon going around the Earth. Gravity is the attraction between bodies with mass. Near the Earth's surface, everything falls with an acceleration of about 9.8 m/s² if there's no air. Newton realized that the same force that makes an apple fall keeps the Moon in orbit: the Moon is «falling» all the time, but it moves sideways so fast that it never crashes. Newton's law of universal gravitation says that the force between two masses is proportional to the product of the masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers: . From it comes the gravitational acceleration at a planet's surface, , which is about 9.81 m/s² on Earth and 1.62 m/s² on the"}, {"t": "Kinematics: velocity, acceleration and straight-line motion", "p": "temas", "a": "cinematica", "k": "From Explorer Kinematics describes how things move: how fast they go and whether they're speeding up or slowing down. When you say «I walked to school in 10 minutes», you're already doing kinematics! Kinematics describes motion without asking what causes it. Velocity says how fast position changes (and in which direction), and acceleration says how fast velocity changes. Moving in a straight line always equally fast is uniform motion (UM); speeding up at a constant rate is uniformly accelerated motion (UAM), like a falling object. In one dimension: average velocity is and average acceleration . In UM (a = 0), . In UAM (constant a): , and . On an x–t graph the slope is the velocity; on a v–t graph the slope is the acceleration and the area under the curve is the displacement. The instantaneous definitions are derivatives: , ; the UAM equations come from integrating twice with constant . I"}, {"t": "Light and sound: electromagnetic and sound waves", "p": "temas", "a": "luz-sonido", "k": "From Explorer Light and sound are waves, like ripples on a pond. Sound is air vibrating and reaching your ears; light travels even through empty space, which is why we see the Sun but can't hear it! And light is super fast: in one second it could go around the Earth more than 7 times. Sound is a mechanical wave: it needs a medium (air, water, solids) and travels at about 343 m/s in air. Light is an electromagnetic wave: it needs no medium and travels at almost 300,000 km/s. In both, the frequency (how many vibrations per second) decides the pitch of a sound or the color of light. Every wave satisfies . Sound is a longitudinal wave (particles vibrate along the direction of travel) and its speed depends on the medium. Light is a transverse wave of electric and magnetic fields; in vacuum it travels at . Visible light is a very thin band of the electromagnetic spectrum, which runs from radio"}, {"t": "Intermediate", "p": "temas", "a": "intermedio", "k": "Electricity, magnetism, real-world forces, the great conservation law, circuits and optics."}, {"t": "Electromagnetism: electric and magnetic fields", "p": "temas", "a": "electromagnetismo", "k": "From Explorer Everything is made of particles with «charge»: some positive and some negative. Like charges push each other away and different ones attract, just like magnets! When charges move through a wire we have electricity, and that electricity can make a magnet. That's how toy motors work. An electric charge creates an electric field around it: a region where other charges feel a force. An electric current (moving charges) creates a magnetic field, like a magnet's. And the other way around: moving a magnet near a wire generates current. That's the idea behind the generators in dams and behind transformers. The electric field is the force per unit charge, , measured in N/C (or V/m). For a point charge, . Field lines leave positive charges and enter negative ones. A current in a straight wire creates a circular magnetic field of magnitude , measured in teslas (T). A charge moving in "}, {"t": "Forces and friction", "p": "temas", "a": "fuerzas-friccion", "k": "From Explorer A force is a push or a pull. Friction is the force that makes things «stick» when they slide: thanks to it your shoes don't slip and a rolling ball eventually stops. On ice there's very little friction, which is why it's so easy to slip! The most common forces are weight (gravity pulling down), the normal force (the push of a surface that supports you), tension (from ropes and cables) and friction, which opposes sliding between surfaces. Static friction keeps something from starting to move; kinetic friction slows it once it's moving, and is usually smaller. To analyze forces you draw a free-body diagram and apply along each axis. Static friction adjusts as needed, up to a maximum ; kinetic friction is . The coefficients μ depend on the materials and have no units. On an incline, the block starts to slide when . The Amontons–Coulomb model (friction proportional to the norma"}, {"t": "The law of conservation of energy", "p": "temas", "a": "conservacion-energia", "k": "From Explorer Energy is like a toy that never breaks or disappears: it just changes costume. On a swing, when you go up you have «height» energy and when you come down it turns into «speed» energy. Eventually you stop because a little bit turns into heat from rubbing against the air. Energy is neither created nor destroyed, only transformed. In a dam, the water's potential energy becomes kinetic as it falls, then turns a turbine and becomes electrical energy. Some always ends up as heat, but if you add up all the forms, the total is the same. If only conservative forces act, mechanical energy is conserved: . With friction, mechanical energy decreases by the work done by friction, which turns into thermal energy: . Counting all forms (thermal, chemical, electrical, nuclear…), the total energy of an isolated system is always conserved. Energy conservation is a consequence of the invariance"}, {"t": "Electric circuits: resistance, voltage and current", "p": "temas", "a": "circuitos", "k": "From Explorer A circuit is a closed path that electricity travels along, like a race track for electrons. The battery is the engine that pushes them, the wires are the track and the bulb is a spot where the electrons «work» and make light. If the track breaks, everything stops! Voltage (V) is the «push» the battery gives; current (I) is how many charges pass each second; resistance (R) is how much a material opposes the current. They're related by Ohm's law: V = I·R. In series, bulbs are one after another and share the current; in parallel, each bulb has its own path (that's how your house is wired). Ohm's law: . Electric power: . Resistors in series: (same current, the voltage is shared). In parallel: (same voltage, the current is shared). Energy used is ; your electricity bill charges it in kilowatt-hours (1 kWh = 3.6 MJ). Kirchhoff's laws solve any network: the sum of currents at a no"}, {"t": "Optics: reflection, refraction and lenses", "p": "temas", "a": "optica", "k": "From Explorer Light travels in a straight line until it hits something. In a mirror it bounces (that's why you see yourself), and when it enters water or glass it «bends». Magnifying glasses and eyeglasses use that bending to make things look bigger or sharper. In reflection, light bounces off at the same angle it arrives. In refraction, light changes direction when it passes from one material to another because its speed changes. Converging lenses (thicker in the middle) bring rays together and form images, as in a camera or your eye; diverging lenses spread them out, as in glasses for nearsightedness. Law of reflection: , measured from the normal. Snell's law: , with refractive index . If light goes from a denser medium into a less dense one and exceeds the critical angle , there is total internal reflection. For thin lenses: and magnification ; a lens's power is measured in diopters, "}, {"t": "Advanced", "p": "temas", "a": "avanzado", "k": "The theories that changed the 20th century: heat and entropy, relativity, quantum mechanics, the atomic nucleus and the first big candidate for a «theory of everything»."}, {"t": "Thermodynamics: the laws and their applications", "p": "temas", "a": "termodinamica", "k": "From Explorer Everything is made of tiny particles that never stop moving. When something is hot, its particles move very fast; when it's cold, more slowly. Heat always goes from hot to cold: that's why your hot chocolate cools down and your hands warm up holding the cup. Temperature measures how fast, on average, the particles of something are moving. Heat is energy that passes from a hot body to a cold one. Engines take advantage of that flow: they burn fuel, the hot gas pushes a piston and part of the heat becomes motion. You never get 100%: some heat is always lost. Zeroth law: if A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with C, they are in equilibrium with each other (this defines temperature). First law: . Second law: the entropy of an isolated system does not decrease; heat does not flow spontaneously from cold to hot and no engine turns all heat into work. Third law: absolute zero"}, {"t": "The theory of relativity", "p": "temas", "a": "relatividad", "k": "From Explorer Albert Einstein discovered something amazing: if you could travel almost as fast as light, your clock would run slower than your friends' clocks on Earth. You'd come back younger than them! He also discovered that planets and stars «sink» space like a bowling ball on a trampoline, and that's gravity. Special relativity (1905) says that light travels equally fast for everyone, no matter how they move. For that to work, time and distances have to change: moving clocks run slower and moving objects get shorter. From there comes the most famous equation, E = mc²: mass is a form of energy. General relativity (1915) explains gravity as the curvature of space and time. Postulates of special relativity: (1) the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames; (2) the speed of light in vacuum, , is the same for everyone. Consequences: time dilation , length contraction , relativ"}, {"t": "Quantum mechanics: the basics", "p": "temas", "a": "cuantica", "k": "From Explorer When we look at tiny things, like atoms, the world gets very strange. Light comes in tiny packets called photons. Particles can behave like waves, like ocean waves. And until you look at them, they don't have an exact place! It's as if they were «a little bit in many places». Quantum mechanics describes atoms and particles. Its key ideas: energy comes in packets (quanta); light and matter behave as both wave and particle; we can only predict probabilities; and there's a limit to how well we can know a particle's position and velocity at the same time (the uncertainty principle). Thanks to it we have lasers, transistors and MRI scans. Planck (1900) proposed that radiation energy is exchanged in quanta . Einstein (1905) explained the photoelectric effect with photons. Bohr (1913) quantized the energy levels of hydrogen, . De Broglie (1924) assigned every particle a wavelength"}, {"t": "Nuclear physics: nuclear energy and reactions", "p": "temas", "a": "nuclear", "k": "From Curious At the center of every atom there's a tiny nucleus with a huge amount of stored energy. If a big nucleus breaks apart, or if two tiny nuclei join together, lots and lots of energy comes out. That's how the Sun shines: by joining hydrogen nuclei! The atomic nucleus is made of protons and neutrons held together by the strong nuclear force. There are two ways to release its energy: fission (a heavy nucleus, like uranium, splits in two) and fusion (two light nuclei, like hydrogen's, join together). Some nuclei are unstable and transform on their own: that's radioactivity. A nucleus's mass is less than the sum of the masses of its nucleons; that mass defect is the binding energy, . Iron-56 and nickel-62 are among the most stable nuclei; that's why heavy nuclei release energy when they split and light ones when they fuse. A typical U-235 fission releases about 200 MeV. Radioactive"}, {"t": "String theory: an introduction", "p": "temas", "a": "cuerdas", "k": "From High school What if everything were made of tiny vibrating strings, like a guitar's? Each way of vibrating would be a different particle: one note would be an electron, another a photon… That's the idea of string theory. But nobody has been able to test it yet! String theory proposes that the fundamental particles are not points but extremely small strings (about m) that vibrate. Different modes of vibration would correspond to different particles, including one that carries gravity (the graviton). To be consistent, the theory needs more dimensions than we see: 10 in total. In string theory, the fundamental object is one-dimensional. A moving string sweeps out a two-dimensional «worldsheet». Strings can be open (with ends) or closed (loops); closed strings always contain a massless spin-2 mode: the graviton. The supersymmetric version (superstrings) is consistent in 10 dimensions; t"}, {"t": "Expert", "p": "temas", "a": "experto", "k": "The frontier: quantum fields, quantum gravity, elementary particles, the whole universe and the geometry of black holes. Even so, every topic starts with an Explorer explanation."}, {"t": "Quantum field theory", "p": "temas", "a": "tcc", "k": "From University Imagine the whole universe is filled with invisible «jellies», one for each kind of particle. When you poke a jelly, a wiggle forms: that's a particle! An electron is a wiggle in the electron jelly, and a photon is one in the light jelly. Quantum field theory says that what's fundamental isn't particles but fields that fill all of space. Particles are small waves or «packets» of energy in those fields. That's why every electron in the universe is identical: they're vibrations of the same field. Forces appear when particles exchange other particles, like the photon. Quantum field theory (QFT) joins quantum mechanics with special relativity. It was needed because, at high energies, allows particles to be created and destroyed, something ordinary quantum mechanics doesn't describe. Its first successful version was quantum electrodynamics (QED), developed in the 1940s by Feyn"}, {"t": "Quantum gravity", "p": "temas", "a": "gravitacion-cuantica", "k": "From University Scientists have two rule books: one for giant things (planets and stars) and another for tiny things (atoms). Both work very well… but they don't agree with each other. At the center of a black hole or at the beginning of the universe you need both at once. Finding a single rule book is one of the great mysteries of physics! General relativity explains gravity and quantum mechanics explains the atomic world, but they're incompatible in extreme situations: the interior of black holes and the instant of the Big Bang. Quantum gravity seeks a theory that unites them. There are several proposals, such as string theory and loop quantum gravity, but none has been confirmed experimentally. Combining , and gives the Planck units: length m and energy GeV. Quantum effects of spacetime are expected at those scales, but they lie about 15 orders of magnitude above the LHC's energy. An "}, {"t": "Particle physics and the Standard Model", "p": "temas", "a": "particulas", "k": "From Curious If you break something into smaller and smaller pieces, you reach atoms. But atoms have parts too: electrons around a nucleus of protons and neutrons. And protons are made of even tinier pieces called quarks! They're like the smallest LEGO bricks in the universe. Particle physics looks for the smallest components of matter. According to the Standard Model, all matter is made of two families: quarks (which form protons and neutrons) and leptons (like the electron and the neutrinos). Forces are carried by bosons: the photon (electromagnetism), the gluons (strong force) and the W and Z bosons (weak force). The Higgs boson, found in 2012 at CERN, explains the mass of many particles. The Standard Model contains 6 quarks (u, d, c, s, t, b), 6 leptons (e, μ, τ and their neutrinos), 4 kinds of gauge bosons (photon, 8 gluons, W±, Z) and the Higgs boson. Quarks have fractional charge "}, {"t": "Origin, evolution and fate of the universe", "p": "temas", "a": "cosmologia", "k": "From Explorer The universe wasn't always the way it is today. A very long time ago everything was squeezed together and very hot, and it began to stretch like a balloon being blown up. As it cooled, atoms formed, then stars, galaxies, planets… and us! And it's still stretching. Cosmology studies the whole universe. We know it's expanding because distant galaxies are moving away from us (Hubble, 1929). If we «rewind» the movie, about 13.8 billion years ago everything was in a very hot, dense state: the Big Bang. It wasn't an explosion at one point, but the expansion of space itself. Today we know the expansion is speeding up because of something we call dark energy. The ΛCDM model rests on three pillars: the Hubble–Lemaître law , the cosmic microwave background (CMB, 2.725 K, emitted about 380,000 years after the beginning) and the abundance of helium and deuterium produced in the first m"}, {"t": "Penrose–Carter diagrams and black holes", "p": "temas", "a": "penrose", "k": "From University A black hole is a place where gravity is so strong that nothing can escape, not even light. It has a «point of no return» called the horizon. Physicists draw special maps of space and time to understand what would happen if you fell in. A Penrose diagram is a map of space and time together, where an entire infinite universe fits on one sheet. Its trick: light always travels along 45° diagonals. That way you can see right away which places can communicate with each other. In a black hole's diagram you can see that, once you cross the horizon, every path into the future leads to the center. A spacetime diagram plots time (vertical) against space (horizontal); light rays go at 45° and massive objects at less than 45° from the vertical. The Penrose–Carter diagram applies a transformation that «shrinks» infinity to a finite distance without changing those angles. That way, fut"}, {"t": "Workbench", "p": "lab", "a": "mesa-titulo", "k": "Read the theory"}, {"t": "Choose an experiment", "p": "lab", "a": "catalogo", "k": ""}, {"t": "Simulation: Projectile launcher", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=lanzador", "k": "Aim, fire and hit the target on Earth, the Moon or Jupiter."}, {"t": "Simulation: Push the box", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=newton", "k": "Newton's second and third laws with friction and skaters."}, {"t": "Simulation: Cart with graphs", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=cinematica", "k": "UM and UAM with live x–t, v–t and a–t graphs."}, {"t": "Simulation: Vector addition", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=vectores", "k": "Drag two vectors and watch their sum, difference and components."}, {"t": "Simulation: Energy track", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=energia", "k": "Potential, kinetic and thermal energy on a ramp."}, {"t": "Simulation: Pendulum", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=pendulo", "k": "Period, energy and damping on different planets."}, {"t": "Simulation: What falls first?", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=caida", "k": "A feather and a hammer, with and without air, on several worlds."}, {"t": "Simulation: Newton's cannon", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=orbita", "k": "From falling to orbiting to escaping the Earth."}, {"t": "Simulation: Inclined plane", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=plano", "k": "Static and kinetic friction; critical angle."}, {"t": "Simulation: Cart collisions", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=choques", "k": "Conservation of momentum in elastic and inelastic collisions."}, {"t": "Simulation: Float or sink?", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=arquimedes", "k": "Archimedes' principle with different materials and liquids."}, {"t": "Simulation: Electromagnetic spectrum", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=espectro", "k": "From radio waves to gamma rays."}, {"t": "Simulation: Sound waves", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=sonido", "k": "See and hear a longitudinal wave."}, {"t": "Simulation: Optical bench", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=optica", "k": "Reflection, refraction, lenses and a prism."}, {"t": "Simulation: Electric field", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=campoE", "k": "Drag charges and watch field lines and potential."}, {"t": "Simulation: Magnetic field", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=campoB", "k": "Compasses around currents; wires that attract."}, {"t": "Simulation: Light-bulb circuit", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=circuito", "k": "Series, parallel and mixed, with moving electrons."}, {"t": "Simulation: Gas in a piston", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=gas", "k": "Temperature, pressure and volume with real molecules."}, {"t": "Simulation: Carnot cycle", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=carnot", "k": "The ideal engine and the limit of the second law."}, {"t": "Simulation: Light clock", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=relatividad", "k": "Time dilation and length contraction."}, {"t": "Simulation: Minkowski diagram", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=minkowski", "k": "Relative simultaneity and the invariant interval."}, {"t": "Simulation: Photoelectric effect", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=fotoelectrico", "k": "Why light is made of photons."}, {"t": "Simulation: Hydrogen atom", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=atomo", "k": "Jumps between levels and spectral lines."}, {"t": "Simulation: Double slit", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=rendija", "k": "Interference with particles one at a time."}, {"t": "Simulation: Particle in a box", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=caja", "k": "Stationary states and superposition."}, {"t": "Simulation: Quantum harmonic oscillator", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=oscilador", "k": "Creation and annihilation operators."}, {"t": "Simulation: Chain reaction", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=fision", "k": "Uranium fission and control rods."}, {"t": "Simulation: Radioactive decay", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=decaimiento", "k": "Half-life of carbon‑14, iodine‑131 and more."}, {"t": "Simulation: Modes of a string", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=cuerda", "k": "Standing waves and the idea behind string theory."}, {"t": "Simulation: Standard Model", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=modelo", "k": "The elementary particles and their forces."}, {"t": "Simulation: Feynman diagrams", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=feynman", "k": "How particles interact."}, {"t": "Simulation: Black hole calculator", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=agujero", "k": "Horizon, temperature and evaporation."}, {"t": "Simulation: From the Planck size to the universe", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=escalas", "k": "62 orders of magnitude on one ruler."}, {"t": "Simulation: Fate of the universe", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=cosmos", "k": "The Friedmann equations with your own parameters."}, {"t": "Simulation: Cosmic calendar", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=calendario", "k": "The whole history of the universe in one year."}, {"t": "Simulation: Penrose diagrams", "p": "lab", "a": "sim=penrose", "k": "The complete map of a black hole."}, {"t": "Parallel lives", "p": "fisicos", "a": "vidas", "k": "Each bar runs from birth to death. Did you know Stephen Hawking was born exactly 300 years after Galileo died?"}, {"t": "Their stories", "p": "fisicos", "a": "todos", "k": "Search By date of birthBy name"}, {"t": "Also influenced physics", "p": "fisicos", "a": "influyeron", "k": "They weren't physicists, but their ideas crossed paths with physics in surprising ways. Leonardo da Vinci1452–1519 · ItalyIn his notebooks he described that friction doesn't depend on contact area and is proportional to weight, two centuries before Amontons. He also studied the optics of the eye and the flight of birds. Charles Darwin1809–1882 · EnglandHis theory of evolution needed a very old Earth. Lord Kelvin, using cooling calculations, gave it only tens of millions of years. When radioactivity (the Earth's internal heat) was discovered, it was shown that the Earth is about 4.5 billion years old. Gregor Mendel1822–1884 · Austrian SilesiaBefore his famous peas, he studied physics at the University of Vienna with Christian Doppler, of the Doppler effect. His statistical analysis of heredity owes a lot to that training. Louis Pasteur1822–1895 · FranceIn 1848 he discovered that certain m"}, {"t": "What physics is your phone made of?", "p": "descubrimientos", "a": "red", "k": "Pick an object and the discoveries it couldn't exist without will light up."}, {"t": "The discoveries", "p": "descubrimientos", "a": "lista-d", "k": ""}, {"t": "Game: put history in order", "p": "descubrimientos", "a": "juego", "k": "Tap the five discoveries in the order they happened, from oldest to most recent. (function(){ // año, nombre, quién, qué (sencillo), por qué importa (avanzado), tecnologías, sim, tema var D = [ [1687,\"The laws of motion and gravitation\",\"Isaac Newton\",\"The same rules explain how an apple falls and how the Moon goes around.\",\"The first universal, mathematical physical theory; it made it possible to predict orbits, tides and the return of comets.\",[\"Satellites\",\"Rockets\",\"Bridges and buildings\",\"Tide prediction\"],\"orbita\",\"newton\"], [1800,\"Direct electric current\",\"Alessandro Volta\",\"The battery: for the first time, an electric current that lasts.\",\"It made possible the experiments of Ørsted, Ampère, Ohm and Faraday; the basis of electrochemistry.\",[\"Batteries\",\"Phone\",\"Electric car\"],\"circuito\",\"circuitos\"], [1831,\"Electromagnetic induction\",\"Michael Faraday\",\"Moving a magnet near a wire "}, {"t": "Myth or fact?", "p": "datos", "a": "mito", "k": ""}, {"t": "Surprising cards", "p": "datos", "a": "tarjetas", "k": "Tap a card to see the explanation. (function(){ var CAT={cua:[\"Quantum\",\"var(--n2)\"],cos:[\"Cosmos\",\"var(--tinta)\"],rel:[\"Relativity and gravity\",\"var(--n3)\"],dia:[\"Everyday physics\",\"var(--n1)\"],nuc:[\"Atoms and particles\",\"var(--n5)\"]}; // categoría, frase gancho, explicación sencilla, explicación avanzada, ancla var C=[ [\"cua\",\"A cat that's «alive and dead» at once\",\"Schrödinger (1935) imagined a cat in a box whose fate depends on an atom that may or may not decay. He invented it to show how strange it would be to apply quantum mechanics to big things.\",\"In practice, decoherence makes macroscopic objects lose superposition almost instantly by interacting with their surroundings.\",\"cuantica\"], [\"cua\",\"You can't know everything at once\",\"Heisenberg's uncertainty principle (1927) says you can't know with perfect precision, at the same time, where a particle is and how fast it's moving. It'"}, {"t": "1. Quantities, units and scientific notation", "p": "bach", "a": "unidades", "k": "The International System (SI) uses the meter (m), kilogram (kg), second (s), ampere (A), kelvin (K), mole and candela. To convert, multiply by factors equal to 1: . Shortcut: from km/h to m/s, divide by 3.6. Scientific notation: . Prefixes: kilo (10³), mega (10⁶), giga (10⁹), milli (10⁻³), micro (10⁻⁶), nano (10⁻⁹). Significant figures: the result can't be more precise than the least precise piece of data. Common mistakeMixing units: using speed in km/h with time in seconds. Convert everything to SI before substituting."}, {"t": "2. Vectors", "p": "bach", "a": "vectores", "k": "A scalar has only magnitude (mass, time, temperature); a vector has magnitude and direction (velocity, force, displacement). Components: , . Magnitude: . Direction: (watch out for the quadrant). To add vectors, add the components separately. Example: a boat sails 30 km east and then 40 km north. What is its displacement? Components: , . Magnitude: . Direction: north of east. Note: the distance traveled was 70 km, but the displacement is 50 km."}, {"t": "3. Uniform motion, uniformly accelerated motion and free fall", "p": "bach", "a": "mru", "k": "UM (constant velocity): . UAM (constant acceleration): , , , . Free fall: it's UAM with downward. From rest: , , . In an upward vertical throw, at the highest point the velocity is zero, but the acceleration is still g. Example: a stone is dropped from a bridge and takes 2.5 s to reach the water. How high is the bridge and how fast does it hit? Given: , , . . (about 88 km/h)."}, {"t": "4. Projectile motion", "p": "bach", "a": "tiro", "k": "It splits into two independent motions: horizontal (UM, ) and vertical (UAM, , ). Flight time (same level): . Maximum height: . Range: . Maximum range is reached at 45°, and complementary angles (30° and 60°) give the same range."}, {"t": "5. Newton's laws and friction", "p": "bach", "a": "dinamica", "k": "Always draw the free-body diagram and choose axes. Apply and . Weight . On a horizontal surface with no other vertical forces, . Friction: (before moving), (while moving). Inclined plane: , the component of weight along the plane . Example: you push a 20 kg box with 100 N across a floor with μₖ = 0.3. What is its acceleration? . . . ."}, {"t": "6. Work, energy and power", "p": "bach", "a": "energia", "k": "Work: . Kinetic energy: . Potential: . Elastic: . Without friction, is conserved. With friction, the difference is the work done by friction. Power: . 1 hp ≈ 746 W."}, {"t": "7. Momentum and collisions", "p": "bach", "a": "momento", "k": "Momentum: . Impulse: . In every collision (isolated system) it is conserved: . Perfectly inelastic collision (they stick together): . Elastic collision: kinetic energy is also conserved."}, {"t": "8. Hydrostatics: pressure, Pascal and Archimedes", "p": "bach", "a": "fluidos", "k": "Density (water: 1000 kg/m³). Pressure , in pascals (Pa). Hydrostatic pressure: ; absolute pressure . Pascal: a pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted equally throughout the fluid. Hydraulic press: . Archimedes: buoyant force . A body floats if its average density is less than the fluid's."}, {"t": "9. Heat and temperature", "p": "bach", "a": "calor", "k": "Scales: ; . Sensible heat: (water: c = 4186 J/kg·°C = 1 cal/g·°C). Latent heat: . Linear expansion: . Heat transfer: conduction, convection and radiation."}, {"t": "10. Electricity: Coulomb, Ohm and circuits", "p": "bach", "a": "electricidad", "k": "Coulomb's law: , . Ohm's law: . Power: . Series: . Parallel: Energy used: ; on your bill, in kWh."}, {"t": "11. Waves and sound", "p": "bach", "a": "ondas", "k": ", . Transverse waves (string, light) and longitudinal waves (sound). Speed of sound in air ≈ 343 m/s at 20 °C. The human ear hears from 20 Hz to 20 kHz. Doppler effect: an approaching source sounds higher; a receding one, lower."}, {"t": "12. Geometrical optics", "p": "bach", "a": "optica-b", "k": "Reflection: . Refractive index: . Snell: . Lenses and mirrors: , magnification . Real image if . Lens power: (diopters, with f in meters)."}, {"t": "Formula sheet", "p": "bach", "a": "formulario", "k": "Print it with Ctrl + P: the site is set up to look clean on paper. TopicFormulas Kinematics Projectile motion Circular motion Dynamics Gravitation Energy Momentum Fluids Heat Gases Electricity Magnetism Waves Optics Modern physics (function(){ var g=9.81; function r(a,b,p){ var x=a+Math.random()*(b-a); var f=Math.pow(10,p||0); return Math.round(x*f)/f; } function e(a,b){ return Math.floor(a+Math.random()*(b-a+1)); } function f2(x){ return PF.fmt(x,2); } var G = { conv:function(){ var v=e(18,144); return {t:\"Unit conversion\",p:\"A car travels at \"+v+\" km/h. What is its speed in m/s?\",r:v/3.6,u:\"m/s\",s:\"\\ \"}; }, vector:function(){ var a=e(3,20), b=e(3,20); return {t:\"Vectors\",p:\"A vector has components Ax = \"+a+\" y Ay = \"+b+\". What is its magnitude?\",r:Math.hypot(a,b),u:\"\",s:\"\\ \"}; }, mru:function(){ var v=e(5,30), t=e(4,60); return {t:\"MRU\",p:\"A cyclist rides at \"+v+\" m/s constant for \"+t+"}, {"t": "A method that works", "p": "recom", "a": "metodo", "k": "To learn physics, play and ask! Do simple experiments at home (with balls, magnets, flashlights), ask «why?» and look for the answer with an adult. Draw what you observe and tell someone about it: if you can explain it, you understood it. First the idea, then the formula. Before memorizing , understand what it means: more force, more acceleration; more mass, less acceleration. Do problems, lots of them. Physics is learned with a pencil in hand. Reading solutions isn't the same as solving. Active recall. Close your notebook and write down what you remember; then compare. It's much more effective than rereading. Spaced review. Review a topic the next day, after a week and after a month. That way it stays in long-term memory. The Feynman technique. Explain the topic in simple words, as if to a child. Wherever you get stuck is what you don't understand. Check units and orders of magnitude. I"}, {"t": "A checklist for solving any problem", "p": "recom", "a": "problemas", "k": "Based on George Pólya's method. Check off each step as you solve. I read the problem twice and underline what's being asked. I make a drawing or diagram (free-body, ray, circuit…). I write down the data with units and convert them to SI. I identify the physical principle: Newton's laws? Conservation of energy? Of momentum? I write the equation in symbols and solve for the unknown before plugging in numbers. I substitute and calculate carefully, watching significant figures. I check: do the units work out? Is the result reasonable? What happens in extreme cases? Start another problem"}, {"t": "Study timer", "p": "recom", "a": "pomodoro", "k": "Study in blocks of total focus followed by short breaks (the Pomodoro technique). During a block: phone away and one task only. Study 25 minStudy 50 minBreak 5 minBreak 15 min 25:00 StartReset Blocks completed today: 0"}, {"t": "Learning path", "p": "recom", "a": "ruta", "k": "What to learn, in what order, from elementary school to graduate school. Open each stage to see its physics topics and the math you need. Your path in physics Each stage builds on the previous one. Math and physics move forward together. Elementary school (Explorer) Curiosity and observation. Physics Math Measuring, simple tables and graphs, fractions. Middle school (Curious) First laws and first formulas. Physics Math High school (High school) Quantitative physics with problems. Physics Math Bachelor's degree (University) Where the equations come from. Physics Math Graduate school (Physicist) Specialization and research. Physics Math"}, {"t": "Resources by level", "p": "recom", "a": "recursos", "k": "Resources for your level are shown first. You can see those for another level with the buttons."}, {"t": "Experiment with your phone", "p": "recom", "a": "experimentar", "k": "Your phone has an accelerometer, gyroscope, magnetometer, microphone and light sensor: it's a pocket lab! The free app phyphox (from RWTH Aachen University) lets you measure an elevator's acceleration, a pendulum's period, the frequency of your voice or a magnet's field. And with Tracker you can analyze videos of motion frame by frame."}, {"t": "Common mistakes when studying physics", "p": "recom", "a": "errores", "k": "Memorizing formulas without understanding what each letter means. Plugging in numbers from the start instead of working with symbols. Forgetting units or mixing systems (km/h with seconds). Not drawing: a good diagram solves half the problem. Reading the solution before trying the problem for at least 15 minutes. Studying everything the night before instead of a little every day."}, {"t": "Olympiads and competitions", "p": "recom", "a": "olimpiadas", "k": "If you like physics, olympiads are the best way to take it further. In Mexico, the National Physics Olympiad organized by the Mexican Physical Society selects the students who represent the country at the Ibero-American Olympiad and the International Physics Olympiad. Problems from past olympiads are excellent practice material."}];
